In Python, getters and setters are methods used to access and modify private attributes of a class in a controlled way. 
They help enforce encapsulation, allowing you to validate or transform data before it's retrieved or updated.

Why Use Getters and Setters?
•	Encapsulation: Protect internal object state from unintended changes.
•	Validation: Ensure only valid data is assigned to attributes.
•	Abstraction: Hide implementation details from the user.
•	Flexibility: Change internal implementation without affecting external code.
Defining Getters and Setters in Python
Python supports a more elegant way to define getters and setters using the @property decorator.
 Example:
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self._name = name  # Conventionally private attribute

    @property
    def name(self):
        print("Getting name...")
        return self._name

    @name.setter
    def name(self, value):
        print("Setting name...")
        if not value:
            raise ValueError("Name cannot be empty")
        self._name = value

# Usage
p = Person("Alice")
print(p.name)     # Calls getter
p.name = "Bob"    # Calls setter
Traditional Getter and Setter (Not Recommended in Python)
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self._name = name

    def get_name(self):
        return self._name

    def set_name(self, value):
        self._name = value
While this works, Pythonic code prefers the @property approach for cleaner syntax and better readability.
 Advanced Tools
•	Descriptors: For reusable getter/setter logic across classes.
•	__getattr__ and __setattr__: For dynamic attribute access or logging.

